
Hi Dave,

I agree that the quality of the technical implementation is owned by the developer, including coding standards, implementation patterns, peer review, and adherence to the team’s design standards. I think where I may not have been clear is that I am separating the business decision model/DHW from the technical implementation design.

In today’s delivery model, developers typically do not interact directly with the business on every requirement. Most of that communication goes through Product, BA, or other resources working closely with the business. If we make the developer responsible for creating and maintaining the business-facing DHW, there will naturally be a lot of back-and-forth to understand the business intent, clarify requirements, update the DHW, get it reviewed, and then begin implementation.

That can significantly slow down story delivery. In fact, that additional manual effort and the difficulty of keeping the DHW synchronized with frequent rule changes is one of the reasons the DHW updates became difficult to maintain consistently in the past.

My recommendation would be that the BA/Product resource, or someone closely engaged with the business, owns the requirement-driven portion of the DHW. They should be able to translate the business requirement into the decision model and walk Dev and QA through it during refinement or sprint planning. That also gives everyone an opportunity to identify ambiguity before development starts.

The developer would still absolutely own the technical implementation, including how the requirement is implemented in DM, the appropriate rule structure, design patterns, coding standards, and technical review. If there are implementation-specific technical details that need to be documented, those can be captured separately or added as part of the development process.

I see the ownership more as:

Business/Product/BA → Business requirement and intended decision behavior
DHW → Clear decision model that removes ambiguity for Dev and QA
Developer → Technical design and implementation
QA → Validation against the requirement/DHW

This way, a developer picking up a story is starting from a reasonably complete and understood requirement rather than having to first reverse-engineer the business intent. The same applies to QA—they have an independent source of expected behavior rather than relying on the implementation to determine what the expected result should be.

I also agree with your point on publishing. We could maintain the upcoming change as a working/versioned DHW during Sprint 0/refinement, use that version for development and testing, and then merge/publish it into the production DHW at go-live. That keeps the production documentation aligned with what is actually live without losing the benefit of having the decision model available before development starts.
